V.I.	Introduction

SCOR’s Azure Site Recovery (ASR) architecture strictly mirrors production by provisioning high-cost Premium SSDs for passive replica disks. To eliminate this dormant capacity waste, the ASR Smart Tiering initiative decouples the target storage by utilizing cost-effective Standard HDDs for background replication. Upon triggering a Disaster Recovery (DR) event, an automated runbook seamlessly upgrades the target disks back to Premium SSD before the application boots. This optimization unlocks significant infrastructure savings while guaranteeing zero impact on active production workloads, application performance, or RPO/RTO SLAs.
This Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) provides a the step-by-step guidelines for the Production team to retrofit existing ASR environments, deploy the automation, and safely execute DR failovers/failbacks.




VI.II.	Prerequisites & Security Setup
To automate the disk SKU conversion without storing credentials, we utilize an Azure Automation Account leveraging a System Assigned Managed Identity.
Critical Architectural Rule: The Automation Account must be deployed in the exact same Subscription as the ASR Recovery Services Vault, and physically located in a DR Region (e.g., West Europe). If not, ASR will not natively see the runbook.
Automation Account
In the Azure Portal search bar, type Automation Accounts and select the service.
Click + Create and fill in the Basics tab:
Subscription: Must match your ASR Recovery Services Vault subscription.
Resource Group: Create a dedicated RG (if not existent in the subscription) e.g. AEaewPprdRrg-ASRAutomation.
Region: West Europe (DR target region).
Automation Account: Name must be AEwPrdAu-ASRAutomation (adapt naming to the region as per SCOR Cloud Naming Convention)
 


Click Next: Advanced.
Under Identity, ensure System assigned is checked (ON). This is mandatory for secure authentication.
 
Click Next: Networking.
Select Public access (required for the runbook to reach the Azure Resource Manager API ).
Select Private access. This complies with SCOR security guidelines to disable public network access. You will configure the Private Endpoint in the subsequent deployment phase based on your standard landing zone VNet.
 
Click Review + Create, then Create.
