5.2. Expected Behavior & Performance Profile
As the Recovery Plan executes, it is critical for the Production and QA teams to understand the expected sequence and the temporary performance profile of the newly provisioned environment:
The Automation Sequence:  ASR will initially provision the target VM and attach the replicated Standard HDDs. Once the VM reaches the Group 1: Post-steps phase, the FinOps runbook takes over. It will power off the VM , upgrade all managed disks to Premium SSD, and power the VM back on. This automated sequence adds approximately 3 to 5 minutes to the total Recovery Time Objective (RTO).
The "Cold Boot" Phenomenon: Upon first accessing the application in the DR region, testers will experience temporary latency. This is standard DR behavior caused by the VM executing a cold boot: RAM is entirely purged, application pools (e.g., IIS, Tomcat) must initialize, and database buffer caches must repopulate.
Lazy Loading: Azure performs background data hydration when initializing disks from snapshots. The first read of a data block may incur micro-latency.

Note for Testers: This initial latency is strictly tied to application caching and hydration, not the FinOps storage tiering. Performance will stabilize within minutes as the application warms up.

5.3. Validating the Smart Tiering Automation
To confirm the FinOps runbook successfully executed its mandate, the Production team must verify the disk SKUs in the target environment.
Navigate to the Target DR Resource Group (e.g., AXXXX-asr).
Select the newly created target VM and click on Disks in the left-hand menu.
 
Verify that the OS and Data disks now show Premium SSD;

Log Verification (Optional): If a failure occurs, navigate to your Automation Account, click on Jobs, and select the latest execution of the ASR-SmartTiering-PremiumUpgrade  runbook. The Output tab will contain detailed logs identifying the specific VMs processed and any Azure API errors encountered.


XXV.VI.	Operations: Cleanup & Failback

After successfully validating the application in the DR region, the environment must be properly decommissioned or failed back. Leaving the DR environment running unnecessarily incurs active compute charges and high-tier Premium SSD costs. The following procedures outline how to safely clean up the environment and restore the FinOps baseline.
6.1. Committing & Reprotecting
Navigate to the Recovery Services Vault > Recovery Plans  > select the target RP.
Click Commit at the top of the pane.
Mechanism: This finalizes the failover. The VM is now permanently running in the West Europe DR  region on Premium SSDs.
 Once committed, click Reprotect to reverse the replication direction (from West Europe back to North Europe).
 
FinOps Action Required:  Once the primary region is restored and you perform the planned failback to North Europe, the West Europe VM will be shut down and its disks will become passive replicas again. You must repeat the steps in Section 4 (Retrofitting Existing ASR Replicas) to downgrade these new West Europe passive disks from Premium SSD back to Standard HDD.

6.3. Post-Cleanup FinOps Verification
Regardless of the failover type, the Production team should perform a final hygiene check to ensure no orphaned resources are generating unexpected costs.
Navigate to the Target DR Resource Group (e.g., AEnPocRg-FinOps-asr).

Filter the resources by Type == Disk.

Verify that the only disks present are attached to the ASR replication engine and have a Storage SKU of Standard HDD.
If any unattached Premium SSDs remain from an aborted failover, manually delete them.
This concludes the standard operating procedure. Your documentation now covers the entire lifecycle: from the architectural concept, to security prerequisites, runbook integration, retrofitting the existing footprint, running the drill, and cleaning up to secure the financial savings.
